INT. NIGHT-SHIFT BAKERY - 4 A.M.

Flour-dusted steel. A dozen racks of rising dough under heat lamps. A radio hissing between stations. The ovens tick as they warm. Everything smells of yeast and, faintly, something sweeter.

SIX BAKERS in white. Five move. One doesn't.

HILDEGARD BRANDT, sixties, the head baker, kneads with the heel of her hand, never looking up. OSKAR WREN, twenties, shapes rolls beside her, a half-beat slower than the rhythm of the room. PAOLA FERREIRA-LIN, thirties, scores loaves with a razor, one clean cut after another. NADEEM ARSLAN, fifties, loads the oven, counts under his breath. RHIANNON POTTS, twenties, sweeps near the back door. And MOSES ABERNATHY-GRAY, forties, stands at the far counter with his back to the others, kneading nothing at all.

The radio drifts to a station playing a slow waltz. Nobody turns it off.

HILDEGARD
Moses. The dough.

MOSES
I'm working it.

HILDEGARD
There's no dough there.

He looks down at his hands. They are slick with flour. They have been pressing and folding an empty counter for an hour.

Paola's razor pauses. Nadeem's lips stop moving.

MOSES
There was.

Nobody answers. The ovens tick. In the back, the door rattles once, as if someone had leaned on it from outside.
